ed the probability of no loss (75% in B) more heavily, even though they mischaracterized the probabilities. Their actual justification seems to be that B offers a higher chance of no loss (75%) compared to A (50%), despite the higher potential loss. Thus, the individual considered the probabilities of the zero outcome and compared the worst outcomes, but ultimately chose B based on the higher probability of avoiding loss.",
